// State of the export dialog (File > Export…).
//
// The dialog edits three stacking settings straight through `SettingsModel`
// (the output folder, the file-name template, and the format / bit depth / JPEG
// quality): a control writes the property and fires `settings-edited()`, the
// same contract as the settings dialog. This global only carries what is not a
// setting: whether the dialog is open, the note about what will be written, the
// template variables and the live preview.
//
// Like the settings contract, the tree holds no default of its own for text that
// Rust owns: `placeholders` and `target-note` are pushed from Rust.
export global ExportModel {
    in-out property <bool> open: false;
    // What the Save button will write ("the full-precision result, retouch
    // included" / "a re-encode of frame_01.jpg: ..."), pushed when the dialog opens.
    in-out property <string> target-note: "";
    // The template variables, space-separated and braced, pushed from Rust.
    in-out property <string> placeholders: "";

    // The user asked for the dialog (menu, Ctrl+E, toolbar). Rust checks that
    // there is something to write, refreshes the preview context, and sets `open`.
    callback request-open();
    // Save with the current dialog values. Rust closes the dialog and writes.
    callback save();

    // The file-name stem a template gives right now (sanitised, no extension) and
    // the placeholders in it that are not recognised (", "-joined, empty when
    // there are none). Pure: Rust answers from a context cached at open time.
    pure callback preview-name(string) -> string;
    pure callback unknown-placeholders(string) -> string;

    public function close() {
        open = false;
    }
}
